\paragraph{Position.}
\textbf{Moving the answer clause to the end produced the largest and most consistent gain.} At the shortest tier, accuracy rose from \positionTwoStartAccuracy{}\% at the start to \positionTwoEndAccuracy{}\% at the end, a paired increase of \positionTwoStartEndChange{} points with an interval of [\positionTwoStartEndChangeLow{}, \positionTwoStartEndChangeHigh{}], \positionTwoStartEndWins{} wins, and no losses. At the middle tier, the same move raised accuracy by \positionFourStartEndChange{} points [\positionFourStartEndChangeLow{}, \positionFourStartEndChangeHigh{}], with \positionFourStartEndWins{} wins and no losses. The sparse longest tier also favored the end over the start by \positionEightStartEndChange{} points, but it contains only \positionEightStartEndDenominator{} pairs. Middle performance was below end performance at the two populated tiers (Table~\ref{tab:position}).

