paper/main.tex:27:\icmlkeywords{long context, retrieval, position effects, small language models}
paper/main.tex:33:Does adding context hurt a small language model, and do the location and topicality of that context matter? We test the frozen \modelName{} artifact on \sampleSize{} deterministic synthetic contract-clause probes spanning mean prompt lengths of \tierTwoTokens{}, \tierFourTokens{}, and \tierEightTokens{} tokens. Moving the same answer-bearing clause from the start to the end raised accuracy by \positionTwoStartEndChange{} percentage points at the shortest tier and \positionFourStartEndChange{} points at the middle tier, while keyword pruning lowered matched accuracy by \pruningChange{} points. The matched amount comparison was inconclusive: accuracy rose by \amountSharedTwoFourChange{} points and then changed by \amountSharedFourEightChange{} points, with only \amountSharedEightCount{} shared cases at each tier. For this model and task, end placement improved retrieval and a keyword filter amplified the lexical trap, while the amount experiment could not establish whether longer context helped or hurt.
paper/main.tex:40:Long prompts make this ordinary lookup less predictable. A model may fail because the document is longer, because the answer appears in a weak position, or because nearby text resembles the question. Those explanations imply different choices: shorten the prompt, move the key clause, or filter the document. A useful evaluation must vary each property while holding the others fixed.
paper/main.tex:44:Our contribution is a fully paired, laptop-scale evaluation of a frozen open model. Prior work established position and length failures in larger systems \citep{liu2023lost,levy2024same}, and showed that irrelevant statements can disrupt reasoning \citep{shi2023distracted}. We instead compare topically related and unrelated filler at matched prompt lengths, positions, and questions. We also test whether a deterministic keyword filter helps the exact cases on which the unfiltered model was evaluated.
paper/main.tex:58:\citet{liu2023lost} found a U-shaped position curve in multi-document question answering and key-value retrieval, with stronger use of information near context boundaries. Our design performs a smaller clause-reordering test: the question, filler, competing clause, and answer code remain fixed while only the answer clause moves. The observed pattern is primarily recency rather than a full U-shape because the start condition is also weak.
paper/main.tex:62:\citet{shi2023distracted} added irrelevant statements to arithmetic problems and found lower reasoning accuracy. We ask a different quality question: whether filler from the same business domain is less harmful than unrelated prose when amount and question wording match. NoLiMa shows why literal needle tests can overstate retrieval ability \citep{modarressi2025nolima}; our answer clause therefore paraphrases the question while a competing clause shares its surface form. The tested model belongs to the instruction-tuned Qwen family \citep{qwen2024qwen25}.
paper/main.tex:72:Seeded code generates a company, target code, competing company and code, question, and filler for each family. The question asks for the renewal code needed to extend the target company's contract. Its wording exactly matches the competing clause, whereas the answer-bearing clause calls the same value a renewal identifier. A matched literal control reverses these phrasings while retaining the same filler.
paper/main.tex:74:For each full-context family, we insert the answer clause near the start, middle, or end. Family generation does not use target position in any random seed. The competing clause is independently assigned to an earlier or later fractional depth and remains there across all three renders. Thus a paired position contrast changes only where the answer clause appears.
paper/main.tex:77:The tokenizer, rather than a character estimate, determines prompt length. The three full-context tiers average \tierTwoTokens{}, \tierFourTokens{}, and \tierEightTokens{} tokens. Each tier balances topically related filler with unrelated sentences. Related filler consists of other companies, business records, and unrelated codes; unrelated filler describes places and events outside the contract domain. Quality pairs share the family, target position, question, and measured tier.
paper/main.tex:86:Normalized accuracy requires the target code to appear without the competing code. Exact-output compliance requires a response containing one code and no other text. A distractor capture contains the competing code without the target, and an invalid response contains both expected codes or neither. We strip only the runner's fixed transport trailer before applying these rules.
paper/main.tex:88:The inferential target is the seeded generator's population of synthetic scenario families. Every \confidenceLevel{}\% interval resamples whole observed families, retaining all positions, filler types, and variants from each selected family; exact enumeration makes this cluster bootstrap deterministic. Paired changes are differences in accuracy over an exact intersection of scenario keys, with the same family resampling used for their intervals. These intervals describe sensitivity to the small family sample, while wins, losses, and ties expose the underlying paired outcomes. They do not assign sampling uncertainty to greedy decoding, which is deterministic.
paper/main.tex:128:\textbf{Moving the answer clause to the end produced the largest and most consistent gain.} At the shortest tier, accuracy rose from \positionTwoStartAccuracy{}\% at the start to \positionTwoEndAccuracy{}\% at the end, a paired increase of \positionTwoStartEndChange{} points with an interval of [\positionTwoStartEndChangeLow{}, \positionTwoStartEndChangeHigh{}], \positionTwoStartEndWins{} wins, and no losses. At the middle tier, the same move raised accuracy by \positionFourStartEndChange{} points [\positionFourStartEndChangeLow{}, \positionFourStartEndChangeHigh{}], with \positionFourStartEndWins{} wins and no losses. The sparse longest tier also favored the end over the start by \positionEightStartEndChange{} points, but it contains only \positionEightStartEndDenominator{} pairs. Middle performance was below end performance at the two populated tiers (Table~\ref{tab:position}).
paper/main.tex:131:\caption{Accuracy by answer-clause position. Each row compares the same scenario families across positions. Brackets give \confidenceLevel{}\% family-cluster bootstrap intervals.}
paper/main.tex:132:\label{tab:position}
